{"name":"Cold Turbulence","hex":"#cfe1ef","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a low temperature.","Causing the air to be cold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A condition of low temperature.","A harsh place; a place of abandonment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cold"},{"term":"turbulence","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state or fact of being turbulent or agitated; tempestuousness, disturbance.","Disturbance in a gas or fluid, characterized by evidence of internal motion or unrest."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turbulence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cold","blocks":[{"text":"Cold is the presence of low temperature, especially in the atmosphere. In common usage, cold is often a subjective perception. A lower bound to temperature is absolute zero, defined as 0.00 K on the Kelvin scale, an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. This corresponds to −273.15 °C on the Celsius scale, −459.67 °F on the Fahrenheit scale, and 0.00 °R on the Rankine scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/330px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold"},{"title":"Turbulence","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics, turbulence or turbulent flow is fluid motion exhibiting chaotic changes in pressure and flow velocity. It is in contrast to laminar flow, which occurs when a fluid flows in parallel layers with no disruption between those layers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbulence"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px